Converting Plain HTML/CSS/JS Mobile Web Apps to Flutter

Comprehensive Guide for Converting Static Web Apps to Flutter (AI Agent Methodology)

This guide is designed to help AI Agents efficiently convert plain HTML/CSS/JavaScript mobile web applications into robust Flutter mobile applications while preserving functionality, design patterns, and user experience.

---

PHASE 1: ANALYSIS & DISCOVERY (Analysis Phase)

Before writing any Flutter code, the agent must thoroughly understand the existing HTML/CSS/JS project structure:

1.1 HTML Structure Analysis (DOM Analysis)
- Examine the main HTML file (index.html) to identify page sections and components
- Map all HTML pages/views (if multiple pages exist) and identify single-page vs multi-page architecture
- Identify semantic HTML elements (header, nav, main, footer, section, article)
- Catalog all forms and input elements (text, email, password, checkbox, radio, select, textarea)
- Extract all interactive elements (buttons, links, dropdowns, modals, tabs, accordions)
- Identify any iframes, embeds, or external content references
- Note any accessibility features (ARIA labels, semantic tags, role attributes)

1.2 CSS Analysis (Styling & Layout)
- Examine CSS files (inline, internal, external stylesheets) and CSS frameworks (Bootstrap, Tailwind, custom)
- Extract color palette: primary, secondary, accent, background, text, borders
- Identify typography: font families, sizes, weights, line-heights
- Document spacing system: margins, padding, gaps (if using modern CSS Grid/Flexbox)
- Catalog all breakpoints and media queries for responsive design
- Identify animations, transitions, and visual effects
- Document box-shadows, border-radius, gradients, and other visual treatments
- Extract icon sets (Font Awesome, Material Icons, SVGs, image sprites)
- Document component styling patterns (Cards, Badges, Buttons, Input fields, etc.)

1.3 JavaScript Functionality Analysis (Behavior & Logic)
- Identify the main JavaScript entry point(s) and overall architecture
- Catalog all event listeners and user interactions (click, submit, change, scroll, etc.)
- Extract data flow: how data is fetched, stored, and updated
- Identify data sources: hardcoded data, API calls (XMLHttpRequest, Fetch API), local storage
- Map all functions and their purposes (data processing, UI updates, validation, formatting)
- Identify state management patterns (global variables, module pattern, simple closures)
- Document any external JavaScript libraries (jQuery, Axios, Chart.js, etc.)
- Identify form validation logic and rules
- Extract any business logic (calculations, filtering, sorting, searching)
- Note any error handling mechanisms

1.4 User Flow & Navigation Analysis
- Create a sitemap/flow diagram showing all pages/views and how users navigate
- Identify persistent UI elements (header, footer, navigation bars)
- Document modal dialogs and overlays
- Identify deeplinks or query parameters used for routing
- Note any redirect logic or conditional page flow
- Document loading states, error states, and empty states

1.5 Data & API Analysis
- Identify all API endpoints being called (method, URL, headers, request/response format)
- Extract request payload examples and response structure
- Document authentication requirements (if any)
- Identify data transformation logic
- Note any data validation or error handling from API
- Document any local storage usage (localStorage, sessionStorage)

